<!--BlockML Documentation — a guide to the three problems BlockML is designed to solve

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.bmldocs.a04_rendering.data.page.docs.CoreUseCases-->
<docs:CoreUseCases xmlns="http://blockml.org/bml"
  xmlns:docs="org.blockml.bml.bmldocs.a04_rendering.data.page.docs"
  xmlns:dpage="org.blockml.bml.docgen.page"
  xmlns:doc="org.blockml.bml.documentation">

  <baseType>
    dpage:DocPage
  </baseType>
  <is>
    A guide to the three problems BlockML is designed to solve
  </is>
  <documentation>
    Scope: the three core BlockML use cases and when to reach for BlockML, grounded in
    org.blockml.bml.documentation.CoreUseCases. Body content is authored in a03 from that source.
  </documentation>
  <references>
    <see type="doc:CoreUseCases" relation="Requires">
      Primary content source — structured project documentation, domain modeling, and AI workspaces.
    </see>
    <see type="doc:Introduction" relation="Prerequisite">
      BlockML goals and the BML/BOM split, read before the use cases.
    </see>
    <see type="doc:EverythingIsABlock" relation="ReadNext">
      Next reading step — the ontological principle these use cases rest on.
    </see>
  </references>
  <properties>
    <title override="true">
      <value>Core Use Cases</value>
    </title>
    <detailLevel override="true">
      <value>org.blockml.bml.docgen.level.Introductory</value>
    </detailLevel>
    <pageType override="true">
      <value>org.blockml.bml.docgen.pagetype.Guide</value>
    </pageType>
  </properties>
  <aggregations>
    <content override="true">
      <composition>
        <dpage:Section heading="One underlying principle">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>BlockML is designed around three use cases that all rest on the same
premise: representing information as a structured, semantic model instead of
unstructured text.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <dpage:Section heading="Structured project documentation">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>A BlockML document can serve simultaneously as documentation for humans,
context for AI systems, and structured input for other tools, because its
structure is explicit rather than implicit. The BlockML model, not any generated
document, remains the source of truth.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <dpage:Section heading="General-purpose domain modeling">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>BlockML describes what something is; render targets decide how that
description manifests, so the same model can produce code, configuration,
documentation, or other domain-specific output. A domain defines its own
vocabulary of Blocks, types, and relationships — BlockML does not prescribe what
a model represents.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <dpage:Section heading="AI workspaces">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>An AI can work directly on a structured BlockML model instead of
repeatedly generating and re-reading unstructured text — inspecting, validating,
and transforming the model while its structure and semantics stay explicit.
Stages, decisions, and intermediate results can become explicit parts of the
model rather than disappearing into conversation history.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <dpage:Section heading="Recognizing your own use case">
          <content>
            <dpage:List>
              <items>
                <dpage:ListItem text="A project's documentation should be part of its structured model, not text attached alongside it." />
                <dpage:ListItem text="A domain's own vocabulary of Blocks needs to be modeled independently of any single output format." />
                <dpage:ListItem text="An AI-driven process needs a persistent, structured model to work on rather than disposable text." />
              </items>
            </dpage:List>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <dpage:Section heading="Continue with the central idea">
          <content>
            <dpage:Navigation>
              <title>Next</title>
              <items>
                <dpage:NavigationItem label="Everything Is a Block" summary="Learn the ontological principle these use cases rest on.">
                  <target>docs:EverythingIsABlockPage</target>
                </dpage:NavigationItem>
              </items>
            </dpage:Navigation>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
      </composition>
    </content>
  </aggregations>
</docs:CoreUseCases>
